  We are testing whether a redesigned Pareto search algorithm can achieve ≥92% coverage of the true 26-point Pareto front within 200 BLIS simulator evaluations, up from the 84.6% (22/26) achieved in iteration 1. The core fix targets 4 missed configurations at mid-range kv_blocks values (3000, 4000) that the previous algorithm's upward-scanning Phase 2 never explored.

  * h-main: The improved 4-phase search replaces iter-1's 5-phase strategy by testing all 15 topologies at both ceiling (kv=5000) and cheapest (kv=2000), then adaptively probing kv=3000 and kv=4000 where preemptions are detected. Prediction: ≥24/26 (≥92%) Pareto configs found within 200 evals. Refuted if coverage stays below 92%, which would indicate the missed configs depend on factors beyond kv boundary mapping.
  * h-dose-response: Runs the improved search at budgets of 50, 100, 150, and 200 evaluations to characterize the coverage-vs-cost curve. Predicted saturating shape: ~60% at 50 evals, ~85% at 100, ~90% at 150, ~96% at 200. A linear rather than saturating shape would challenge iter-1's finding that scheduler and maxrun knobs are irrelevant to Pareto structure.
  * Practical details: Each BLIS evaluation takes ~0.15s (full 200-eval search ~30s). The reference Pareto front (26 configs from 180-point exhaustive sweep) is the ground truth. Code changes are confined to search.py. Iter-1's 84.6% baseline serves as the control, so no separate control arm is needed.

