────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  SUMMARY
────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

  We are testing whether the 4-phase adaptive Pareto search algorithm (from iteration 2) generalizes beyond its original qwen3-14b/rate=100 regime to three additional regimes (Llama-70B/rate=100, qwen3-14b/rate=200, qwen3-14b/rate=50), or whether its 100% Pareto coverage was overfit to one setting. The key code change makes the KV-cache ceiling adaptive (kv=max instead of hardcoded kv=5000) so the algorithm discovers model-specific ceilings automatically.

  * H-main predicts ≥90% Pareto coverage across all 4 regimes within 200 evaluations each. The critical fix is replacing the hardcoded kv=5000 ceiling (qwen3-14b-specific) with kv=max(KV_VALUES)=10000, since probes revealed Llama-70B's effective ceiling is at kv=3000, not 5000.
  * H-robustness predicts ≤10 percentage-point coverage variation between seed=42 and seed=123 across all 4 regimes, testing whether the deterministic evaluation order makes results stable despite workload randomness.
  * Total experiment: 8 search runs (4 regimes × 2 seeds × 200 evals each) plus 4 exhaustive baselines (~180 configs each) to compute reference Pareto fronts. Expected wall time ~4-5 minutes total based on prior timing (~30-35s per search run).
  * Confirmation: ≥90% Pareto coverage in all 4 regimes (seed=42) and ≤10pp difference between seeds. Refutation: coverage drops below 90% in any regime, which would indicate the 4-phase structure has model- or rate-specific blind spots (diagnostic checks: missing high-kv Pareto configs, missing non-FCFS/low-maxrun configs, or unexpectedly large reference fronts).
  * Phase 2 intermediate fill is expanded from [3000, 4000] to [3000, 4000, 5000], and Phase 4 budget freed by removing redundant kv=7500/10000 (already in Phase 1) is repurposed for kv=5000/7500 at remaining topologies — no net increase in evaluation budget.

