  * Two experiment arms: (1) h-main tests the MoE model (Llama-4-Scout-17B-16E-Instruct-FP8-dynamic) at rate=100, seeds 42/123, predicting >=90% Pareto coverage — the MoE model exercises completely different latency model code paths (expert routing, FLOPs scaling) but the search algorithm should be architecture-agnostic since it only observes output metrics.
  * h-robustness tests Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct at rate=100, seeds 42/123, predicting >=90% Pareto coverage despite: (a) TP=8 configs causing panics (NumHeads 28 not divisible by 8) that must be gracefully handled, (b) zero preemptions at any KV value making Phase 2 boundary mapping a complete no-op, and (c) a degenerate Pareto front where kv=2000 is always cheapest-optimal (no KV diversity).
  * Each arm runs the adaptive search (200 evals) against an exhaustive baseline (~180 valid configs for MoE, fewer for Qwen2.5-7B). Success criterion is >=90% Pareto coverage for both arms across both seeds. Failure would indicate the algorithm's phase structure is over-fitted to dense model dynamics — specifically, check whether MoE's different ITL (~21ms) shifts Pareto-optimal topologies, or whether the algorithm wastes too many evals on redundant KV variations in the KV-insensitive small-model regime.
  * Preflight: 'go build -o blis main.go' compiles the simulator. Wall time is estimated at ~35s per MoE search run and ~25s per Qwen2.5-7B search run based on design-probe timings (~0.15s and ~0.1s per eval respectively). All unlocked parameters (gpu_memory_utilization=0.9, admission=always-admit, preemption=fcfs, routing=round-robin) are fixed across conditions to isolate the architecture variable.

